---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";
import { stores } from "../data/stores";
import { chromeWebStoreUrl, isStoreLive } from "../site.config";

const storeCountries = new Intl.ListFormat("en-GB", { type: "conjunction" }).format(
  stores.map((store) => store.country),
);
---

<Base title="Changelog" description="What each version of the Across Amazon extension does.">
  <PageIntro title="Changelog" lede="One entry per version. Version numbers follow the extension’s manifest." />
  <div class="container page-body">
    <article class="prose">
      <section class="release" aria-labelledby="v0-2-0">
        <header class="release-head">
          <div class="release-title">
            <h2 id="v0-2-0">0.2.0</h2>
            <p class="release-label">Faster checks without tabs</p>
          </div>
          <p class="release-status">
            Prepared for the Chrome Web Store, not published yet. Until it is, the store offers 0.1.0.
          </p>
        </header>

        <h3 id="v0-2-0-what-changed">What changed</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>No tab per page.</strong> Each store’s pages are downloaded with your Amazon session and read in a
            hidden page of the extension, which runs none of Amazon’s scripts and loads none of its images or ads.
            Downloaded pages do not appear in Chrome’s history, and Amazon can tell these downloads apart from your own
            visits. A background tab opens only for a page that cannot be read that way, for example a robot check, a
            download that fails or takes longer than 15 seconds, a page the extension does not recognise or that leads
            to another Amazon store, a page that shows another delivery destination than the one the store is set to, a
            store that refuses to change its delivery location from the extension, or an offers list it cannot load. A
            page in a tab is read as soon as its content has arrived, without waiting for Amazon’s images and ads to
            finish loading.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Three stores at a time.</strong> A check of nine stores takes about 15 seconds; in tests, 0.1.0
            took one and a half to four minutes for the same check. Stores with equal totals keep the order of the
            settings.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>New permission: offscreen.</strong> It lets the extension open one hidden page of its own during a
            check, which downloads and reads the other stores’ pages. Chrome shows no warning for it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No false “not found” on a slow page.</strong> A page that took more than 15 seconds to finish
            loading, as the first page of a check sometimes did, was taken for a store that does not list the product,
            which cost a search and another page. Now a download that takes longer than 15 seconds is read in a
            background tab instead, which has 20 seconds and reads the page as soon as its content is there.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Robot checks reported as such.</strong> When Amazon shows a robot check instead of a page, the store
            is listed as <span class="nowrap">“Could not read the page”</span> with a note that names it, instead of
            being read as an unavailable listing or a search without results. A robot check on a download is tried once
            in a background tab. When the tab shows it too, the check opens no other page on that store and is not
            stored, so <span class="nowrap">“Check again”</span> checks every store again.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Stop.</strong> <span class="nowrap">“Stop checking”</span> also stops the downloads in progress and
            closes any tab it opened.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A time limit.</strong> A check stops after 4 minutes. A store not finished by then is listed as{" "}
            <span class="nowrap">“Could not read the page”</span> with “The check ran out of time before this store was
            read.” Such a check is not stored, so <span class="nowrap">“Check again”</span> checks every store again.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="release" aria-labelledby="v0-1-0">
        <header class="release-head">
          <div class="release-title">
            <h2 id="v0-1-0">0.1.0</h2>
            <p class="release-label">First release</p>
          </div>
          <p class="release-status">
            Published on the {isStoreLive ? <a href={chromeWebStoreUrl}>Chrome Web Store</a> : "Chrome Web Store"} on{" "}
            <time datetime="2026-10-22">2026-10-22</time>.
          </p>
        </header>

        <h3 id="v0-1-0-what-it-does">What it does</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Only when you ask.</strong> Opening the popup only reads the product page you are on. A check starts
            when you press <span class="nowrap">“Check 9 other Amazons”</span>.
          </li>
          <li><strong>Ten stores.</strong> {storeCountries}.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>The same product.</strong> Each store is looked up by the product’s ASIN, Amazon’s product number.
            When a store does not list it, the extension searches for the brand and model number when the title has
            one, then for the title, brand and selected variant.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Careful matching.</strong> A listing found by searching carries a confidence score and, where the
            listings name them, is checked against your colour, pack and size. Weak matches are left out. Open the
            listing and <a href="/faq/#check-match">check it is the same product</a> before you buy.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Background tabs.</strong> Stores are read one at a time, in background tabs that never take focus.
            Each tab closes as soon as it has been read. In tests, a check of nine stores took one and a half to four
            minutes. The pages it opens are ordinary visits: they appear in Chrome’s history and, if you are signed in
            to Amazon, may appear in your Amazon browsing and search history.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>One destination.</strong> So that every price is quoted for the same place, the extension may set
            each other store’s delivery location to the country in the “Deliver to” box of the page you started from,
            and leaves it that way. The store you started from is never changed.
            You can <a href="/how-it-works/#changing-it-back">change it back</a> in that store’s “Deliver to” box.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Delivered total.</strong> Stores are ranked by item price plus shipping and import fees, as shown on
            Amazon’s own pages. When a store shows no import fees for your destination, the result says so, because
            duties may still be due on delivery.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Other sellers’ offers.</strong> When a store’s own listing shows no price for your destination but
            offers <span class="nowrap">“See All Buying Options”</span> and does not say the item is currently
            unavailable, the check reads that list and quotes the cheapest delivered of the new offers it could read
            and convert, naming the seller Amazon shows for it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Exchange rates.</strong> Totals are converted to one currency at the European Central Bank’s daily
            reference rates, with Frankfurter as a fallback.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Kept locally.</strong> Your settings and results stay only in your browser. A stored result holds
            the product details, including the “Deliver to” text and the page address, and each store’s prices, with the seller’s name when another seller’s offer was quoted. A
            finished check is reused for 30 minutes. An older result is deleted only when exactly the same check runs
            again; otherwise it stays until you remove the extension or clear its storage.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Settings.</strong> Choose the currency prices are shown in, and switch any store off.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 id="v0-1-0-what-it-never-does">What it never does</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>No checkout.</strong> It never adds to cart, checks out or buys anything.</li>
          <li><strong>No credentials.</strong> It never reads your passwords or your cookies.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>No changes to your addresses.</strong> A store’s delivery location is set to a country, never to an
            address. Your saved addresses stay as they are.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No price tracking.</strong> It compares prices only when you press the button and never watches
            them over time.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>You buy on Amazon.</strong> Purchases always happen on Amazon’s own product
            pages. <span class="nowrap">“Open on …”</span> takes you to the listing, and you decide.
          </li>
          <li><strong>No account or server.</strong> It has no account, no analytics and no server of its own.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <p class="release-policy">
        The <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> sets out what the extension reads, stores and sends.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .release + .release {
    margin-top: 3em;
  }

  .release-head {
    padding-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
  }

  .release-title {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px 16px;
  }

  .release-title h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
    font-size: var(--text-section);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }

  .release-label {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 2px 10px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--color-tint);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: 650;
  }

  .release-status {
    margin-top: 12px;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  .release-policy {
    margin-top: 2.5em;
    padding-top: 20px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
</style>
